user_prompt:
Variables are listed below in random order. Use the exact id strings.
[
  {
    "id": "V01",
    "description": "up to high school or university degree",
    "states": "high|uni",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "V02",
    "description": "the size of the city the individual lives in, recorded as either small or big",
    "states": "small|big",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "V03",
    "description": "male or female",
    "states": "M|F",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "V04",
    "description": "Age of people using transport",
    "states": "young|adult|old",
    "state_count": "3"
  },
  {
    "id": "V05",
    "description": "employee or self-employed",
    "states": "emp|self",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "V06",
    "description": "the means of transport favoured by the individual",
    "states": "car|train|other",
    "state_count": "3"
  }
]

Task: propose a causal order prior. Earlier variables are potential causes or upstream conditions; later variables are downstream effects, measurements, symptoms, decisions, or aggregate outcomes.

Return one JSON object with this schema:
{
  "ordered_nodes": ["exact id 1", "exact id 2", "..."],
  "confidence": 0.0,
  "high_confidence_pairs": [
    {"before": "exact id", "after": "exact id", "confidence": 0.0}
  ],
  "rationale": "brief, no more than 80 words"
}

Constraints: ordered_nodes must contain every provided id exactly once; do not invent nodes; if evidence is weak, still provide the best semantic prior and lower the confidence.

raw_response:
{
  "ordered_nodes": ["V03", "V04", "V01", "V05", "V02", "V06"],
  "confidence": 0.68,
  "high_confidence_pairs": [
    {"before": "V03", "after": "V05", "confidence": 0.82},
    {"before": "V04", "after": "V05", "confidence": 0.76},
    {"before": "V04", "after": "V06", "confidence": 0.78},
    {"before": "V05", "after": "V06", "confidence": 0.7},
    {"before": "V02", "after": "V06", "confidence": 0.66}
  ],
  "rationale": "Sex and age are most upstream demographic attributes. Education typically precedes employment status, and employment may influence transport choice. City size is a contextual factor that can shape transport options, but transport preference is the most downstream outcome here. This is only a semantic prior, not a claim of true causality."
}
